diff --git a/docs/build-validation.md b/docs/build-validation.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0143139
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/build-validation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+# Frontend build and browser validation
+
+The production build now succeeds on Node 22.11.0 with `CI=true`, without a
+legacy crypto option, disabled linting, skipped preflight checks or dependency
+file edits. The reviewed compatibility update pins `react-scripts` 5.0.1, whose
+locked webpack is 5.111.1. React and React DOM remain 16.10.2.
+
+Compiled-browser testing also exposed the inherited React Bootstrap beta's map
+dialog marking itself `aria-hidden`. Pinning React Bootstrap 1.6.8 (still the
+Bootstrap 4 family) replaces its faulty react-overlays 1.2.0 with 5.2.1. The map
+title labels the dialog. Pin/unpin controls are labeled buttons, and closing the
+map cancels its pending delayed-render timer. These are frontend changes only.
+
+The resulting frontend lock also resolves `prop-types` to 15.8.1. Its manifest
+range remains unchanged. Other direct application dependency versions remain
+unchanged; build-tool and modal transitive dependencies change. Backend locks
+and runtime source are unaffected. This does not clear the wider dependency
+review: Create React App is in [maintenance mode](https://react.dev/blog/2025/02/14/sunsetting-create-react-app),
+and the inherited UI dependencies still require review before public deployment.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+Use an explicitly selected Node/npm installation. The local Windows `npm.cmd`
+wrapper resolves npm 6.14.15, while the npm CLI bundled beside Node 22.11.0 is
+10.9.0. This validation uses the latter for dependency resolution/installation,
+with normal peer checks and lifecycle scripts disabled. It retains lockfile
+format 1 rather than converting unrelated package records to another format.
+
+In `react-frontend`, install with `npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund`,
+then set the required settings from [browser freshness](browser-freshness.md).
+Run `CI=true npm run build`, using your shell's environment syntax. The tested
+build uses `/stats-api`, 250 ms polling and a 1000 ms request deadline. These are
+local acceptance settings, not a production profile. The bundle assumes hosting
+at `/`; the proxy must provide the configured same-origin API prefix.
+
+Root `npm test` passes 110 contracts. The frontend's seven React DOM tests use
+`/stats-api`, 100 ms and 50 ms and cover unavailable/empty data, expiry/recovery,
+unmount cancellation and the accessible map-close lifecycle.
+
+## Compiled-browser scope
+
+The local acceptance fixture `test/fixtures/browser-server.cjs` serves the built
+assets and real HTTP API on a random IPv4 loopback port. It uses synthetic
+in-memory storage; set `BROWSER_SCENARIO_FILE` to an absolute test JSON file.
+Supported modes are `ready`, `empty`, `unavailable`, `expired`, `hanging` and
+`stop`. This is a test fixture, not a deployment server. It needs no database,
+node credential or validator signing key.
+
+A separate headless Edge profile passes populated data, keyboard pinning, an
+accessible map with two reported-node markers, empty data, storage failure and
+recovery, expired storage, request timeout and recovery. Real Axios/XHR, compiled
+JavaScript, local fonts/flags and API freshness headers participate. The test
+observes no page exceptions, missing assets or foreign-origin requests. One
+aborted API request is expected when the deliberate hang hits its deadline.
+The browser and fixture are stopped after testing.
+
+This is local synthetic acceptance at a desktop viewport. Actual efsn telemetry,
+per-node freshness, collector limits, full storage/browser/TLS integration,
+production dependency review, service supervision and broader device/browser
+coverage remain launch work. A successful build alone is not public readiness.
